        "laureates": [
          "Adrian R. Krainer",
          "C. Frank Bennett"
        ],
        "citation": "for antisense correction of SMN2 splicing to treat spinal muscular atrophy"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B007",
    "B024"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B008 joins a precise disease mechanism to a clinically effective intervention for a devastating inherited neuromuscular disorder. Krainer and Bennett offer a focused attribution spanning the relevant splicing biology and antisense-therapeutics development. The sustained clinical implementation makes this a mature demonstration that RNA-based mechanism can decisively alter disease.",
    "case_against": "Important contributions to SMN disease genetics, oligonucleotide chemistry, clinical trials, and the specific therapeutic program extend beyond the two proposed laureates. Its historical distance and breadth of generalization across RNA medicine are also less settled than for some longer-established foundational discoveries.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "linnarsson-sten",
        "point": "Linnarsson ranked B024 first, emphasizing its exceptional therapeutic consequences in diabetes and obesity while noting difficult boundaries between foundational GLP-1 biology and pharmaceutical development.",
        "response": "I agree that B024 has extraordinary medical importance and a deeply mature physiological basis. The same crowded path from hormone discovery through medicinal optimization, however, makes the capped attribution less clean than for B008; it remains a strong alternative rather than my preferred configuration."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "wahren-herlenius-marie",
        "point": "Wahren-Herlenius ranked B007 first because TNF blockade combines a compelling inflammatory mechanism with durable, broad clinical transformation.",
        "response": "The Feldmann-Maini pairing is indeed unusually coherent and TNF blockade has established a major therapeutic principle over decades. I give B008 a narrow preference because its intervention directly corrects a defined pathogenic splicing defect and provides an especially clear bridge from molecular mechanism to treatment, while recognizing that B007 is at least as mature historically."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "perlmann-thomas",
        "point": "Perlmann ranked B001 first, stressing the fundamental and mechanistically mature account of cellular adaptation to endoplasmic-reticulum stress.",
        "response": "The UPR is a profound, durable cellular discovery with a strong Mori-Walter attribution. I place the clinically decisive and disease-specific result in B008 ahead this round, without treating immediate therapeutic impact as the sole measure of merit."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the listed pair adequately captures the distinct foundational, chemical, and clinical contributions required for the successful SMN2 therapy.",
      "How heavily to weigh a direct therapeutic transformation against older, more broadly foundational discoveries."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
